v_1 = 0 \text{ m s}^{-1}
v_2 = 12 \text{ m s}^{-1}
t = 3.0 \text{ s}
a = \frac{(12.0 \text{ m s}^{-1})}{3.0 \text{ s}}
= 4.0 \text{ m s}^{-2}

The first equation of uniformly accelerated motion helps in determining the velocity of an object after a certain time when the acceleration is given. As you know, by definition
Acceleration (a) = \frac{\text{Change in velocity}}{\text{Time taken}} = \frac{v_2 - v_1}{t_2 - t_1}
If at t_1 = 0, v_1 = v_0 and at t_2 = t, v_2 = v. Then
a = \frac{v - v_0}{t} \tag{2.5}
v = v_0 + at \tag{2.6}

