Let a body of mass m tied to a string be rotated anticlockwise in a vertical circle of radius r about a point O. As the body rotates in the vertical circle, its speed is maximum at the lowest point P. It goes on decreasing as the body moves up to Q, and is minimum at the highest point Q. The speed goes on increasing as the body falls from Q to P along the circular path.
The forces acting on the body at P are weight of the body 'mg' and the tension T_1 of the string in the direction as shown in Fig. 4.9. Similarly, the forces acting on the body at Q are mg and the tension T_2 in the direction shown in Fig. 4.9. If v_1 and v_2 be the velocities of the body at P and Q, respectively, we have at P:
